; SEROUT v7.1 - listing (addresses relative to SO = RAM+&0C5), 82 bytes
;
                 ; =====================================================================
                 ; SEROUT v7.1 - software UART transmitter 8N1 for the Sharp PC-1500(A),
                 ; 19200 bps, normal or inverted polarity (chosen in the installer).
                 ; CPU LH5801 @ 1.3 MHz, output PC7 (&F008 bit 7).
                 ;   normal (TTL):  idle and bit "1" = high, start bit and "0" = low
                 ;   inverted:      idle and bit "1" = low,  start bit and "0" = high
                 ;
                 ; Call from BASIC (as in v6.x):
                 ;   CALL SO          send 1 character from TX+0
                 ;   CALL SO,N        send N characters from TX+0.. (1..255; 0 = 1, >255 = 255)
                 ; The variable N is NOT changed (returns with C=0).
                 ; TX buffer: 255 bytes at the start of a memory page (TX = RAM+&300).
                 ;
                 ; Timing. Cycle counts are those of MAME's LH5801 table (1 cycle = 0.769 us
                 ; at 1.3 MHz); 19200 bps = 67.7 cycles per bit. The calibration on a real
                 ; PC-1500 measured that a bit of SEROUT v7.0 took 73.3 real cycles where
                 ; the MAME table gives 67, i.e. about 6.3 cycles more. v7.1 therefore has a
                 ; shorter loop:
                 ;   one data bit = TB = 50 + PAD cycles (MAME), PAD = 8, 10..15:
                 ;   TB = 58, 60..65; default 61. The second calibration (v7.1, TB = 62)
                 ;   measured 52.92 us per bit (68.8 real cycles), so TB = 61 gives about
                 ;   52.15 us. Its 2-bit intervals fit a model in which a port write takes
                 ;   1.9, SHR 3 and a taken branch into another 256-byte page about 1.5
                 ;   cycles more than MAME (the bit loop lies across RAM+&100; start bit +
                 ;   bit 0 have two such branches less than two data bits, 3.1 cycles).
                 ; - The bit counter is UL with LOP (8 bits), instead of a sentinel bit and
                 ;   BII A,&FF + BZR (7 cycles less).
                 ; - Both branches (bit 0 / bit 1) take 33 cycles; the port write comes 3
                 ;   cycles later for a 1 bit than for a 0 bit (moves single edges by 2.3 us,
                 ;   does not add up).
                 ; - The character counter is XL (the buffer starts a page): CPI XL,N at the
                 ;   end of the stop bit; N is written into it at the start (the code changes
                 ;   one of its own bytes, at SO+CMPN+1). UH is free for the timing pad.
                 ; - Start bit = TB (19 fixed cycles + the same PAD), last data bit = TB
                 ;   (+11 cycles after the loop), stop bit = 55 + 11*KT cycles (KT = 1: 66).
                 ; Writes to PC7 are the pseudo instructions MARK / SPACE (FD op imm):
                 ;   MARK  #(Y) = FD OM VM   normal ORI #(Y),&80, inverted ANI #(Y),&7F
                 ;   SPACE #(Y) = FD OS VS   normal ANI #(Y),&7F, inverted ORI #(Y),&80
                 ;
                 ; Load address: SO = RAM+&0C5 (&40C5).
                 ; Symbols filled in by BASIC: TP = high byte of the TX buffer address,
                 ; CP = high byte of RAM+&100 (page of CMPN), KI = idle before the first
                 ; start bit, KT = stop bit, P1 P2 P3 = timing pad, OM/VM/OS/VS = polarity.
                 ; =====================================================================
00  FD BE                RIE                 ; no interrupts (timing!)
02  84                   LDA  XH
03  8B 08                BZS+ SMALL          ; N = 0..255
05  BF 80                BII  A,&80          ; CALL SO without a variable: X = ROM address (XH>=&80)
07  89 07                BZR+ N1             ;   -> 1 character
09  B5 FF                LDI  A,&FF          ; N = 256..32767 -> 255
0B  8E 05                BCH+ NOK
0D  04           SMALL:  LDA  XL
0E  89 02                BZR+ NOK            ; N = 1..255
10  B5 01        N1:     LDI  A,1            ; N = 0 -> 1 character
12  AE CP 12     NOK:    STA  (CP,&C5+CMPN+1-&100)   ; N into CPI XL,N (SO = RAM+&0C5)
15  48 TP                LDI  XH,TP          ; X = TX buffer (start of a page)
17  4A 00                LDI  XL,0
19  58 F0                LDI  YH,&F0         ; Y = &F008 (port C)
1B  5A 08                LDI  YL,&08
1D  FD OM VM             MARK #(Y)           ; PC7 = idle (mark) ...
20  6A KI                LDI  UL,KI          ; ... for about 1 bit before the first start bit
22  88 02        IDLE:   LOP  UL,IDLE
24  45           CHAR:   LIN  X              ; A = character, X++
25  FD OS VS             SPACE #(Y)          ; start bit (space)
28  P1 P2 P3             PAD  3,P            ; start bit: the same pad as a data bit ...
2B  6A 07                LDI  UL,7           ; ... plus 19 cycles; UL = 8 data bits for LOP
2D  8E 00                BCH+ S1
2F  38           S1:     NOP
30  D5                   SHR                 ; C = bit 0
31  83 05        OUT:    BCS+ ONE            ; send the bit in C
33  FD OS VS             SPACE #(Y)          ; bit 0 (space)
36  8E 04                BCH+ NXT
38  FD OM VM     ONE:    MARK #(Y)           ; bit 1 (mark)
3B  38                   NOP                 ; (both branches 33 cycles)
3C  P1 P2 P3     NXT:    PAD  3,P            ; timing pad: 8 or 10..15 cycles
3F  D5                   SHR                 ; C = next bit
40  88 11                LOP  UL,OUT         ; 8 bits
42  6E 00                CPI  UL,0           ; last data bit as long as the others
44  F9                   REC                 ; (11 cycles)
45  FD OM VM             MARK #(Y)           ; stop bit (mark)
48  6A KT                LDI  UL,KT          ; length of the stop bit
4A  88 02        TDLY:   LOP  UL,TDLY
4C  4E 00        CMPN:   CPI  XL,0           ; N characters sent? (N written at the start)
4E  99 2C                BZR- CHAR           ; next character
50  F9                   REC                 ; C=0: variable N unchanged
51  9A                   RTN
